### Program Overview

This C++ program solves a problem related to tree structures and graph traversal. It takes input representing a tree and outputs a numbering scheme for its nodes. The solution involves finding the diameter of the tree, constructing a path along this diameter, and then assigning numbers to the nodes based on their positions relative to this path.

### Classes and Structs

#### None

The provided code does not define any classes or structs. All functionality is encapsulated within the `main` function and some helper functions.

### Global Variables and Standalone Functions

#### Global Variables

- `N`: An integer representing the number of nodes in the tree.
- `V[100010]`: An array of vectors, where `V[i]` contains the indices of the neighbors of node `i`.
- `visited[100010]`: An array used to keep track of the distance from the starting node during BFS.
- `path`: A vector storing the path from one end of the diameter to the other.
- `ans`: A vector storing the first numbering scheme.
- `ans2`: A vector storing the second numbering scheme.

#### Standalone Functions

- `longest(int v)`: Performs a breadth-first search (B
